From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: DTLS 1.2 AES-128-GCM records sealed by the transport, a burst's together

OpenSSL seals each application record on its own: one request to the crypto engine, GHASH on the CPU and its record
layer around them, about a quarter of a data channel's CPU at full rate. With a DtlsRecordSealer installed
(rtc::SetDtlsRecordSealer), a DTLS 1.2 connection that negotiated ECDHE-ECDSA- or ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256
derives its write key and salt from the session (RFC 5246 key expansion) and proves them: one of OpenSSL's own
application records must open with them, or OpenSSL keeps every record. Once the peer's first application record
has arrived, so OpenSSL no longer resends its Finished, the transport builds the records itself; the takeover
happens under the SSL lock, so no SSL_write is under way.

From then on the transport gives the epoch's sequence numbers, and every nonce comes from one counter with two
disjoint ranges. OpenSSL starts the explicit nonce at a random value and advances it by one with every record of the
key, as it does the sequence number, so their difference is one number for the whole epoch; the transport checks
that on every record OpenSSL writes before the takeover (a record that does not follow it leaves all records to
OpenSSL). OpenSSL's counter cannot be moved, and OpenSSL seals a record of its own (an alert: close_notify, a fatal
alert) under it before the transport sees the result. So at the takeover the next 65536 values of that counter stay
OpenSSL's, and the transport's nonces begin after them: the same difference plus 65536, plus the sequence number.
No nonce is used for two encryptions, neither on the wire nor inside the process. Before OpenSSL could leave its
range (64 values before the end) the transport stops calling it: it reads nothing more and the connection ends. A
sequence number has 48 bits; the transport seals no record past the last one.

OpenSSL also keeps its own sequence numbers after the takeover, so its alert would repeat one the transport gave.
It is not sent as written: the transport opens it with the write key and sends its content as its own next record,
so the peer still gets an authenticated alert.

A record is numbered and sealed by the thread that sends it: a batch of the transport's queue in one call of the
sealer, before its first send (Transport::prepareBurst), so a retry sends the same sealed bytes; a record sent
outside a burst alone. One thread at a time sends a transport's records, so their order on the wire is the order of
their numbers. Records the sealer does not take are sealed with EVP. Renegotiation is off already
(SSL_OP_NO_RENEGOTIATION). Other suites, DTLS versions and TLS libraries are unchanged.
